        <div data-figma-id="I214:503;387:333" class="d-d91e7c1b3">
          <div class="d-a66e68297">
            <div class="d-08bedd5fe">
              <p class="d-c043cbf2b">03 / POSITIONING</p>
              <p class="d-6e1c41d3a">Every provider promised centimeter accuracy.</p>
              <p class="d-d9b1f8e58">
                It started with a simple question no one could answer honestly:
              </p>
              <p class="d-4f57ae6ee">
                if your data is accurate in a lab, why does it fail in a city?
              </p>
              <p class="d-231d67a81">
                I spent months researching competitors, mapping customers, and studying the real
                physics of positioning in dense urban environments.
              </p>
              <p class="d-231d67a81">
                What I found was uncomfortable: the entire industry was selling a lab result as a
                real-world guarantee.
              </p>
              <p class="d-49256a283">
                This is how I turned that truth into a sixty-second film — and built a story that
                makes the most complex problem in geospatial data impossible to ignore.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="I214:503;387:334" class="d-991ada67d">
          <div class="d-39edfa3a4">
            <div class="d-08bedd5fe">
              <p class="d-9c4e5ea3c">02 / SAMENSTAD</p>
              <p class="d-5a725ed49">It started with a small question I couldn't answer:</p>
              <p class="d-f5e9b84ca">
                if everyone can see that something in a city is broken, why does it stay broken?
              </p>
              <p class="d-a0a25c90c">
                I spent months looking into how reporting actually works in a Dutch city — and found
                that the problem was never the form.
              </p>
              <p class="d-c184c6c4f">It was everything that happens after it.</p>
              <p class="d-a515a9bc8">
                This is how I designed an app where a street, a municipality and a company can
                finally fix something together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="I214:503;387:335" class="d-2f538eb52">
          <p class="d-9c4e5ea3c">01 / CYCLOINTEL</p>
          <p class="d-f5e9b84ca">Everything Cyclomedia needed to know was already public.</p>
          <p class="d-a0a25c90c">
            The announcements sat in plain sight for days, in a pile with everything else that also
            looked like it might matter.
          </p>
          <p class="d-c184c6c4f">
            They didn't need better eyes. They needed something that could decide what was worth
            looking at.
          </p>
          <p class="d-a0a25c90c">
            What does &quot;important&quot; actually mean — and can you teach a machine to know?
          </p>
          <p class="d-a515a9bc8">This is a story about teaching a system to tell the difference.</p>
        </div>
